# Auto-Scaling Policy — Preventing OOMKilled

## Goal
Spread load across multiple instances instead of relying on one container, and scale up before hitting the memory limit — not after.

## 1. Set realistic resource limits
```yaml
resources:
  requests:
    memory: "256Mi"
    cpu: "250m"
  limits:
    memory: "512Mi"
    cpu: "500m"
```
Base these numbers on real usage data from monitoring, not guesses.

## 2. Horizontal Auto-Scaling (add more instances automatically)
```yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: app-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: app-deployment
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: memory
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 30
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300
```

**Key points:**
- `minReplicas: 2` — never rely on a single instance
- Scale up at 70% memory usage, before hitting the OOM limit
- Scale up fast, scale down slowly (avoids flapping — constantly adding/removing instances)

## 3. Avoid duplicate/broken requests during scaling
```yaml
readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 10
terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30
```
This makes sure traffic only goes to healthy containers, and containers finish in-flight requests before shutting down.

## Summary
Realistic limits + auto-scaling at 70% + health checks = the system spreads load automatically instead of one container crashing repeatedly.
